In Equation (8.3.11), we learned that for an infinite geometric series
\begin{equation*}
S = a + ar + ar^2 + \cdots + ar^{n-1} + \cdots\text{,}
\end{equation*}
if \(|r| \lt 1\text{,}\) then
\begin{equation*}
S = \frac{a}{1-r}\text{.}
\end{equation*}
This result provides a quick way to determine the value of an infinite geometric series that converges.
In this exercise, we explore why the infinite geometric series fails to converge when \(r = 1\text{,}\) \(r = -1\text{,}\) and \(|r|>1\text{.}\)
If we consider the finite geometric series that results when \(a = 1\) and \(r = 1\text{,}\) we get the sum
\begin{equation*}
S_n = 1 + 1 + \cdots + 1\text{,}
\end{equation*}
where there are \(n\) terms in the sum.
Compute \(S_2\text{,}\) \(S_3\text{,}\) and \(S_4\text{.}\) What is the general formula for \(S_n\text{?}\)
Explain why the sequence \(S_n\) does not converge to a finite value. Contrast this with, for example, our work in Example 8.3.5.
Next, consider the finite geometric series that results when \(a = 1\) and \(r = -1\text{,}\) which is the sum
\begin{equation*}
S_n = 1 - 1 + 1 - 1 + \cdots + (-1)^{n-1}\text{,}
\end{equation*}
where there are \(n\) terms in the sum. (We view the first “\(1\)” as resulting from “\(1 \cdot (-1)^0\)”.)
Compute \(S_2\text{,}\) \(S_3\text{,}\) \(S_4\text{,}\) and \(S_5\text{.}\) What do you observe?
Explain why the sequence \(S_n\) does not converge to a finite value. Contrast this with, for example, our work in Example 8.3.5.
Now consider the geometric sum with \(a = 1\) and \(r = 2\text{,}\) so
\begin{equation*}
